<!--
  jt-portable-detect — portable executable detection for Linux and macOS
  File:    zz-906200-jt_portable_nix_rules.xml
  Range:   906200-906299
  Licence: AGPL-3.0-or-later    Jason Tools Co., Ltd.

  Why this is separate from the Windows rules
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  On Windows a portable program has a shape: a .exe that writes no registry
  entries. Linux and macOS have neither convention. "Executable" is one
  permission bit, and alerting on it produces noise — in a measured estate,
  most of the u+x files under /root were .pem and .key certificates.

  So these rules do not ask "is it executable". They ask three narrower
  questions, in increasing order of how much they prove:

    1. Was an existing file *made* executable?  A package manager installs
       with the right mode already set; a person who downloads a tool and
       runs chmod +x is doing the portable-executable thing by definition.

    2. Did something with the execute bit appear where programs do not
       belong — /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm?

    3. Did a program actually *run* from one of those places?  This is the
       only one of the three that is evidence of execution rather than of
       preparation, and it needs auditd. See the note above rule 906210.

  What file integrity monitoring cannot tell you
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A syscheck event carries a path, a mode, a size and hashes. It does not
  carry the file's magic bytes, so it cannot distinguish an ELF binary from
  a shared memory segment that happens to be mode 0777. That is not a
  hypothetical: rule 906203 raised 53 level-12 alerts in one four-hour
  burst on one host, every one of them a POSIX IPC object under /dev/shm
  created by an application at 0777. The rule was right about the mode and
  wrong about what it meant. The burst shape is what an application restart
  looks like, so it recurs.

  Levels here therefore follow the strength of the evidence, not how
  alarming the location sounds. A level 12 alert that is wrong every time
  teaches people that level 12 means nothing.

  Load order
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Rule files load in filename order, and among sibling rules the last one
  loaded wins. The zz- prefix keeps these from being shadowed by a customer
  file that also hangs off 550/553/554.

  Requires
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Parent rules 550 (modified), 553 (deleted), 554 (added) — built-in FIM.
  Agent-side collection: see the pack's agent/agent.conf, installed as the
  portable-detect agent group. Rules 906210 and 906211 additionally need
  auditd with agent/jt-portable.rules loaded.
-->

<group name="linux,macos,jt_portable,">

  <!-- Base rule for a file event in an area a user can write to.
       Level 1, not 0, deliberately: level 0 ends rule evaluation, which
       would silence every rule below this one as well as unrelated
       built-in detections. Measured on this project. -->
  <rule id="906200" level="1">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp|dev/shm|home/[^/]+|root|Users/[^/]+)/</field>
    <options>no_log</options>
    <description>File event in a user-writable area on Linux or macOS (base rule)</description>
  </rule>


  <!-- 1. An existing file was made executable.
       The strongest of the file-based signals: software installed from a
       package arrives with its mode already correct and never needs this.

       changed_fields is the rule-side name; the JSON alert calls it
       changed_attributes. Writing the JSON name here fails silently. -->
  <rule id="906201" level="8">
    <if_sid>906200</if_sid>
    <field name="changed_fields" type="pcre2">permission</field>
    <field name="perm" type="pcre2">x</field>
    <description>[WARN] A file in a user area was made executable - $(file) perm: $(perm) owner: $(uname)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1222.002</id>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_chmod,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- 2. Something executable appeared where programs do not belong.
       Subdirectories are included: an installer unpacking to
       /tmp/xxxx/setup is the common shape, and matching only the first
       level misses it. The exclusions at the end of this file are what
       keeps that affordable. -->
  <rule id="906202" level="10">
    <if_sid>550,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp)/</field>
    <field name="perm" type="pcre2">x</field>
    <description>[WARN] Executable appeared in a temporary directory - $(file) perm: $(perm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_tmpexec,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    /dev/shm is its own rule and its own level.

    In principle a binary dropped on a memory filesystem is worse than one
    in /tmp: it never touches a disk and is gone on reboot. In practice
    /dev/shm is where every library that uses POSIX shared memory puts its
    segments, semaphores and mutexes, and those are routinely created 0777.
    File integrity monitoring cannot tell them apart from a program.

    So this reports at a level that matches what it actually knows: a file
    with the execute bit appeared on a memory filesystem, which is worth a
    look. Execution from /dev/shm is rule 906211, which has auditd behind
    it and is a level 12 because it is evidence rather than a suggestion.
  -->
  <rule id="906203" level="6">
    <if_sid>550,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/dev/shm/</field>
    <field name="perm" type="pcre2">x</field>
    <description>Executable-mode file appeared on a memory filesystem - $(file) size: $(size) owner: $(uname)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1564</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_shm,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- 3. File formats that mean "portable" on these platforms.
       AppImage is the Linux equivalent of a Windows portable build. -->
  <rule id="906204" level="8">
    <if_sid>906200</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)\.(AppImage|run|bin|jar)$</field>
    <description>[WARN] Portable-format file appeared in a user directory - $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_file,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- macOS. A .app is a directory, so FIM reports the binary inside it. -->
  <rule id="906205" level="6">
    <if_sid>906200</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)^/Users/[^/]+/(Downloads|Desktop|Documents)/.*\.(dmg|pkg)$</field>
    <description>Disk image appeared in a macOS user directory - $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_file,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="906206" level="8">
    <if_sid>906200</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)^/Users/[^/]+/(Downloads|Desktop|Documents|Public)/.*\.app/Contents/MacOS/</field>
    <description>[WARN] Application bundle appeared in a macOS user directory - $(file)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_file,</group>
  </rule>


  <!--
    4. Execution, via auditd.  Optional, and the only part of this file that
       proves a program ran rather than that one was prepared.

       Needs auditd running on the agent with agent/jt-portable.rules
       loaded, and the agent reading /var/log/audit/audit.log. Without it
       everything above still works.

       Worth knowing before switching it on: watching /home for execute
       access on a busy multi-user host produces a lot of audit records.
       Start with /tmp, /var/tmp and /dev/shm, which is what the shipped
       rules file enables by default.

       Parent 80700 is the built-in auditd group and is level 0. That is
       fine for if_sid; it would not be for if_matched_sid, which is why
       the correlation rule below hangs off 906210 rather than off 80700.
  -->
  <rule id="906210" level="10">
    <if_sid>80700</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.key">jt_portable_exec</field>
    <description>[WARN] Program executed from a user-writable area - $(audit.exe) uid: $(audit.uid)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_exec,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="906211" level="12">
    <if_sid>80700</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.key">jt_portable_tmpexec</field>
    <description>[HIGH] Program executed from a temporary or memory filesystem - $(audit.exe) uid: $(audit.uid)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1564</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_exec,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Scripts run through an interpreter.  Added 2026-09-08.

    The blind spot this closes: a `-w /tmp -p x` watch fires on execute
    access to a file under /tmp. Running `bash /tmp/tool.sh` never triggers
    it, because the thing being executed is /bin/bash — /tmp/tool.sh is
    merely opened and read. Rules 906210 and 906211 therefore see nothing,
    and a large share of portable tooling is exactly this shape: a shell,
    Python or Perl script rather than a compiled binary.

    This rule reads the interpreter's arguments instead of its path. Wazuh
    decodes an execve record into audit.execve.a0, a1, a2 ...; when the
    executed program is an interpreter and one of its arguments points into
    a temporary or memory filesystem, a script living there just ran.

    It does not overlap 906210/906211, which fire when the *executed image*
    is under those paths; here the interpreter is the image and the script
    is an argument.

    ★ Both conditions read audit.execve.*, and that is not a stylistic
      choice. auditd emits two records per execution and Wazuh decodes them
      as two separate events: the SYSCALL record carries audit.exe,
      audit.key and audit.uid, the EXECVE record carries audit.execve.a0,
      a1, a2. Nothing joins them, so a rule naming a field from each can
      never match. Measured with wazuh-logtest on 2026-09-08 — the first
      draft of this rule paired audit.exe with audit.execve.a1 and was
      exactly that kind of rule, one that loads cleanly and never fires.

    ★ Known limit: this reads a1, the first argument, which covers
      `bash /tmp/tool.sh`. It does not cover `bash -c ...`, where the path
      lands in a2 or later. Extending it means one more rule per argument
      position, which is not obviously worth it — the -c form usually
      carries an inline command rather than a dropped script.

    Prerequisite, and how to check it. This needs execve records, which the
    -p x watches alone do not produce. Many hardened estates already audit
    execve for CIS; run

        auditctl -l | grep execve

    to see. If nothing comes back and you want this rule, add to
    jt-portable.rules:

        -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/bin/bash -k jt_portable_interp

    one line per interpreter you care about — filtering by interpreter
    keeps the volume far below auditing every execve on the host.
  -->
  <rule id="906213" level="10">
    <if_sid>80700</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.execve.a0" type="pcre2">^(?:/\S*/)?((ba|da|k|z|tc|c)?sh|python[0-9.]*|perl|ruby|php|node|lua[0-9.]*)$</field>
    <field name="audit.execve.a1" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp|dev/shm)/</field>
    <description>[WARN] Script executed through an interpreter from a temporary or memory filesystem - $(audit.execve.a1) by $(audit.execve.a0)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_exec,jt_portable_interp,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- A program that runs once from /tmp may be an installer. One that runs
       repeatedly from there is something living in a directory nothing is
       supposed to live in. -->
  <!--
    ★ 2026-09-08：移除了原本的 <same_source_ip />。

    auditd 事件是主機本機的行程執行，沒有網路對端，srcip 欄位並不存在，
    而 same_source_ip 要求該欄位存在且相等 ── 條件永遠不成立，這條規則
    等於被自己關掉。佐證：Wazuh 內建的兩個 audit 規則檔
    （0365-auditd_rules.xml、0850-audit_rules.xml）合計使用 same_source_ip
    的次數是 0，而本檔案的 Windows 雙胞胎規則（906150／906151）也沒有用，
    其註解已載明「frequency 預設即限定同一 agent」。
  -->
  <rule id="906212" level="12" frequency="6" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_sid>906210</if_matched_sid>
    <description>[HIGH] Repeated execution from user-writable areas on one host - $(audit.exe)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_portable_exec,</group>
  </rule>


  <!--
    Exclusions. These must come after the rules they override, because
    Wazuh takes the last sibling that matches.
  -->

  <!-- Package and configuration managers doing their normal work. -->
  <rule id="906220" level="0">
    <if_sid>906201,906202,906204</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp)/(apt|dpkg|rpm|yum|dnf|snapd?|flatpak|ansible|chef|puppet|salt)[-_.]</field>
    <description>Temporary file from a package or configuration manager (normal)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Certificates, keys and documents are not programs, whatever their
       mode. Measured: /root/wazuh-certificates/*.pem ships with u+x. -->
  <rule id="906221" level="0">
    <if_sid>906201,906202,906203,906204</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)\.(pem|key|crt|cer|csr|conf|cfg|ya?ml|json|xml|log|txt|md)$</field>
    <description>Certificate, configuration or document file, not a portable executable</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Monitoring agents, including this one. -->
  <rule id="906222" level="0">
    <if_sid>906201,906202,906204</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp)/(ossec|wazuh|zabbix|nagios|nrpe|telegraf|datadog|newrelic)</field>
    <description>Temporary file from a monitoring agent (normal)</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    POSIX inter-process communication objects under /dev/shm.

    shm_open() and sem_open() create ordinary files here, and applications
    routinely create them 0777. Every one of the 53 level-12 alerts this
    pack raised in a week on one host was one of these: shared memory
    segments, mutexes and event objects belonging to a mail application.

    This matches the naming conventions those objects follow, both the
    vendor-specific prefixes and the _shm / _mtx / _sem style suffixes.

    Be clear about the trade-off: an attacker who names a dropper
    something_shm lands in this exclusion. That is accepted here because
    the alternative — 53 false level-12 alerts a week — trains people to
    ignore the rule, which defeats it just as thoroughly and more quietly.
    Execution from /dev/shm is still caught by 906211, which this does not
    touch, and which auditd backs with evidence a filename cannot forge.
  -->
  <rule id="906224" level="0">
    <if_sid>906203</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)^/dev/shm/(sem\.|\.?(com|org|net)\.|pulse-shm-|PostgreSQL\.|mono\.|lttng-|ust-|aio-|\.nvidia|\.io\.)|^/dev/shm/.*_(shm|mtx|sem|evt|lock|mutex|cond|queue)$</field>
    <description>POSIX shared memory or semaphore object, not a program</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Execution from a temporary directory that is a configuration manager
    doing its job.

    Added 2026-09-08. Until then rules 906210 and 906211 carried no
    exclusions at all, while the file-based rules carried four. That
    asymmetry is not a small one: Ansible stages every module it runs into
    a temporary directory and executes it there, so a single playbook run
    against a host produces one level 10 alert per task. Salt, Chef, Puppet
    and RPM post-install scriptlets have the same shape.

    These match the staging directory names those tools generate, not the
    name of the process doing the work — an attacker chooses a process
    name, but not the path Ansible builds for itself.

    Note this deliberately does not exclude /dev/shm: no configuration
    manager stages work there, so an execution from a memory filesystem
    stays at level 12 whatever the path looks like.
  -->
  <rule id="906230" level="0">
    <if_sid>906210,906211</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp|home/[^/]+/\.ansible/tmp|root/\.ansible/tmp)/(ansible|salt|chef|puppet|rpm-tmp|dnf|yum|pip|cloud-init)[-_.a-z0-9]*/</field>
    <description>Execution from a configuration manager's own staging directory (normal)</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Package manager post-install scriptlets. RPM and dpkg both write a
    script to a temporary path and execute it as the last step of every
    package installation, which is exactly the shape rule 906211 is looking
    for and exactly not what it is meant to catch.
  -->
  <rule id="906231" level="0">
    <if_sid>906210,906211</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">^/(tmp|var/tmp)/(rpm-tmp\.|dpkg-|apt-|deb-|tmp\.[A-Za-z0-9]{6,}$)</field>
    <description>Package manager post-install scriptlet (normal)</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    906225-906229 are left for site-specific exclusions. For example:

    <rule id="906225" level="0">
      <if_sid>906201,906202,906204</if_sid>
      <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/tmp/mycompany-deploy-</field>
      <description>Excluded: corporate deployment tool, change request CR-1234</description>
    </rule>
  -->

</group>
